{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~1–3 EUR early horizon, widening to ~5–12 EUR beyond ~day 10). Apply a downward level shift, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/mean-reversion mis-specification: during sustained sell-offs the model assumes rebound/sideways too quickly; true series continues drifting lower. Add a negative drift term when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear downtrend (e.g., majority of daily changes negative).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow with lead time; the model’s long-horizon path is too elevated and too smooth. Use a ramped correction (small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 6–15, largest cut days 16–30).",
    "Overreaction to short-term upticks: after brief rebounds in history, the model projects higher follow-through than realized. Damp positive impulses (reduce sensitivity to the last few up days) and enforce stronger mean reversion downward in bearish regimes.",
    "Volatility/level mismatch: model keeps forecasts clustered in low-70s even when realized breaks into mid/low-60s; incorporate regime switch—if spot breaks below recent support (e.g., below 10–20d low), shift forecast band down rather than reverting to prior range."
  ]
}
